  "title": "<p class=\"ql-align-center\"><strong>The Role of&nbsp;Socioeconomic Status&nbsp;in the Relationship Between&nbsp;Parenting and Child Externalizing Behavior</strong></p><p><br></p>",
  "abstract": "<p class=\"ql-align-justify\">Child externalizing behaviors bear intricate associations with parenting strategies. However, parenting strategies do not exert the same influences on every child; sociodemographic variables affect the range of expression of child outcomes. The present study sought to elucidate whether associations between parenting and externalizing behaviors differ depending on socioeconomic status (SES). Measures of externalizing behavior, SES, and parenting were administered to 348 primary caregivers of youths in middle childhood (52.9% females, <em>M</em><sub><em>age</em></sub> = 9.81, <em>SD</em><sub><em>age</em></sub> = 0.66). Race and ethnicity were primarily split between white, Black or African American, and Latino/a participants (30.2%, 29.6%, and 24.7%, respectively). Family-level SES was found to moderate the relationship between parenting and relational aggression such that youths of low family-level SES exhibited more relational aggression when reared with positive parenting, while youths of high family-level SES exhibited less relational aggression when reared with positive parenting. Results indicate that many parenting strategies affect behavior similarly for children of high and low SES, but positive parenting affects relational aggression differently for children of high and low family-level SES. Replication and cautious interpretation of these results are necessary.</p><p><br></p>",
